<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huaihai Road</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Avenue Joffre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huaihai Road is a major shopping street in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Under the French Concession it was known from 1915 as the Avenue Joffre, after the French marshal, and became the main street of a fashionable residential district. It was renamed in 1950 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaihai-campaign">Huaihai campaign</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huaihai-road.jpg" alt="Huaihai Road" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livelikerw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Huaihai_Rd._2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Huaihai Road joins three streets that were once separate. Middle Huaihai Road, the main section and the former Avenue Joffre, runs for 5,500 metres, mostly through the former Luwan District, which was merged into Huangpu in 2010, and on into Xuhui and Changning. West Huaihai Road, 1,510 metres long, forms part of the boundary between Changning and Xuhui, and East Huaihai Road, 373 metres long, was the boundary between Huangpu and Nanshi before those districts merged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The middle section was built in 1901 as Rue Sikiang, named after the Xi River, partly inside the expanded French Concession and partly as an extra-settlement road beyond it. It became Route Paul Brunat five years later, and the rest of the road came into the concession in 1914. Joseph Joffre attended an official renaming ceremony during a visit in 1922. The eastern part held many shikumen houses, the western part large houses and mansion blocks, and in the 1920s Russians fleeing the Communist revolution made the street a commercial centre of European-style shops, cafes and restaurants.</p><p>After the Japanese took over the foreign concessions in 1941, the street was renamed Taishan Road in 1943. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> government then named it Lingsen Road after Lin Sen, the former President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, who had died in 1943, and the Communist government, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1949, gave it its present name a year later.</p><p>East Huaihai Road was built between 1874 and 1898 as Rue Ningpo, just north of the moat of the old city wall, on land of the Siming Gongsuo, an association of families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a> that had premises and a graveyard there from 1797. French attempts to take the land and move the graves led to violent confrontations in 1874 and 1898, and the road was named after Ningbo as part of the settlement. The western section was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai-municipal-council">Shanghai Municipal Council</a> as Jordan Road and lined with apartment buildings and houses of Chinese and foreign elites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Middle Huaihai Road is one of the two major shopping streets of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> with Nanjing Road, lined with boutiques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> stores, malls, hotels and restaurants. Line 1 of the Shanghai Metro runs beneath it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huaihai Road” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its tree-lined streets, such as the <b>Avenue Joffre</b>, became a favoured residential district.</blockquote><a href="/state/shanghai-french-concession">Shanghai French Concession</a></li></ul></section>
